  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on which lottery is more left-skewed, i.e., assigns more probability to better outcomes. In this case, Lottery A has a higher probability of the best outcome (0 Euros: 99.9% vs 99.8%), so it is more left-skewed and would be preferred by the reason. However, the individual chose B and justified this by the smaller loss amount despite higher probability. The information considered by the individual (loss size and probability trade-off) partially overlaps with the inputs needed for skewness (probabilities and outcomes), but the rule used—prioritizing smaller loss magnitude—is different from the skewness rule. The individual does not reference the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes or any concept resembling distributional asymmetry. Therefore, while some data overlap exists, the reasoning process and decision rule differ fundamentally. The justification aligns more with minimizing maximum loss or expected loss (though expected loss is equal: -6 Euros in both cases) than with skewness.",
